Split the apps surfaces: a most-used shelf, a complete index

The phone stage's shelf and the Apps screen were doing overlapping
jobs. They now do different ones.

The shelf is a most-used row. Use counts live in the store and are
recorded on every open — on the head unit too, where the rest of the
simulation is inert — so the ordering is real rather than an editorial
guess. It is seeded with the prototype's own history so the row is
ordered from the first frame. Ties fall back to catalogue order and
the sort is memoised on the counts, so one press never reshuffles a
shelf the driver is reading.

The Apps screen becomes the complete index of what the panel can open:
the car's three bands plus the phone's projected apps. Pressing one
hands the stage a destination, which meant lifting the active app out
of CastScreen and into the store. Held entries announce themselves
through the same safety state the car's own restricted tiles use.

Four bands do not fit the 900px panel at the old proportions, so the
screen now divides the height it has instead of overflowing it: each
band takes an equal share and its tiles fill that share. Band labels
drop to body size and the tile padding tightens; the height that gives
back goes to the hit targets, which stay at 107px, well past the 76dp
floor. Measured, not estimated — the index now fits exactly.

Tests: three reducer checks on the use counts, four UI checks covering
the index contents, that no band is pushed off the glass, the
index-to-stage route, and that the shelf reorders by use without
churning on a single press. verify-ui located the parked band as "the
last band", which the new band broke; bands now carry their own
modifier so the test names what it means.

